# =============================================================================
# Mise en Place — environment reference
# =============================================================================
# Copy to `.env` for local development. Production values live in the hosting
# platform's variable store (Railway), never in this file and never in `.env`.
#
# Every variable below is read through src/lib/server/env.ts, which defaults
# each one to '' or a working fallback. NOTHING here fails at boot when it is
# missing — a missing STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET does not crash the app, it makes
# webhooks return 200 and record nothing (issue #485). Treat the [required]
# markers as real obligations, not as things the app will remind you about.
#
# Legend
#   [required]      the app is broken without it
#   [required-prod] only required on a deployed environment
#   [optional]      feature degrades to a "not configured" state, no 500s
#   [worker]        must ALSO be set on the worker service, not just web
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# QUICK START — a complete local .env is these five lines
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:yourpassword@127.0.0.1:5432/mep_test
#   AUTH_SECRET=dev-secret-not-for-production
#   GEMINI_API_KEY=dummy
#   STORAGE_DRIVER=local
#   UPLOADS_DIR=uploads
#
# Everything else defaults sensibly. Add a section only when you are actively
# working on that feature. Local setup walkthrough: .claude/skills/verify/SKILL.md
# =============================================================================


# --- Database -----------------------------------------------------------------
# [required] [worker]
# Direct connection — used by the app, drizzle-kit migrations and the pg-boss
# worker. Railway: reference the Postgres service, or read it with
#   railway variables --service Postgres --kv
# Local: postgresql://postgres:yourpassword@127.0.0.1:5432/mep_test
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:[password]@postgres.railway.internal:5432/railway

# [optional] Session Mode pooler URL for the Drizzle ORM runtime queries.
# Recommended for multi-replica deployments. Falls back to DATABASE_URL when unset.
# DATABASE_POOL_URL=

# [required-prod]
# Ignored entirely for localhost / 127.0.0.1 URLs — pgSslConfig() disables SSL
# for those before reading this variable, so there is no need to set it locally.
#
# Only 'require' and 'verify-full' are understood; any other value logs a warning
# and falls back to 'require'. There is no 'disable'.
#
# 'require' encrypts the connection but does NOT verify the server certificate
# (rejectUnauthorized: false) — see src/lib/server/db-ssl.ts and issue #523.
# Railway's managed Postgres cert is issued for CN=localhost only, so its SAN
# never matches the connecting hostname and true verify-full hostname checking
# cannot pass against it. Set DATABASE_CA_CERT to the pinned root-ca cert to
# verify the chain (still no hostname check) if you need stronger guarantees.
DATABASE_SSL_MODE=require
#
# DATABASE_CA_CERT takes the CA certificate itself (a PEM starting with
# '-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----') or a path to a .crt file — never a mode name.
# Putting 'verify-full' here instead of in DATABASE_SSL_MODE makes startup fail.
# DATABASE_CA_CERT=

# [optional] Fail fast when the database host is unreachable. Without a connect
# timeout the driver waits indefinitely and every page that queries the DB hangs
# instead of rendering a degraded state.
DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# [optional] Server-side cap on any single statement, in ms.
DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000


# --- Database: tests ----------------------------------------------------------
# Do NOT set either of these in a normal local .env.
#
# DB-backed suites INSERT and DELETE real rows, so they run only against a local
# host (localhost / 127.0.0.1 / ::1 / host.docker.internal). Against anything
# else they skip with a loud notice — see tests/helpers/db-gate.ts.
#
# There are two connections in play during a test run:
#   DATABASE_TEST_URL — what the test file uses to seed rows
#   DATABASE_URL      — what the application code under test uses
#                       (saveReviewedInvoice, the Stripe webhook handlers, ...)
#
# If those two disagree, seeds land in one database and application writes land
# in the other: suites fail with empty result sets, and a hosted DATABASE_URL
# gets written to. The gate therefore requires BOTH to be local, so the only
# safe configuration is pointing them at the same database — which means a
# single local DATABASE_URL is enough and DATABASE_TEST_URL is unnecessary.
#
# Set DATABASE_TEST_URL only to keep `pnpm dev` on a different local database
# than the one the tests wipe:
# DATABASE_TEST_URL=postgresql://postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/mep_test

# Escape hatch: run DB-backed tests against a NON-local database, and skip the
# both-must-be-local check. Destructive — only for a throwaway/branch database,
# never for production.
# ALLOW_REMOTE_DB_TESTS=1

# Set by CI when DB secrets are known to be configured: turns a disabled gate
# into a hard failure instead of a silent skip.
# REQUIRE_DB_TESTS=1


# --- Auth (Auth.js / SvelteKitAuth) -------------------------------------------
# [required] Session/JWT signing secret. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Use a throwaway value locally and a real random one in production — rotating
# it invalidates every existing session.
AUTH_SECRET=

# [optional] Google OAuth — Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials.
# Authorized redirect URI: {origin}/auth/callback/google (Auth.js's SvelteKit
# adapter always mounts at /auth, regardless of where any route file lives).
# Unset, the Google button 404s; the credentials flow still covers signup/login.
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=


# --- File storage -------------------------------------------------------------
# [required] [worker]
# 'local'   — writes files to UPLOADS_DIR on local disk. Correct for development.
# 'railway' — writes to a Railway Bucket (S3-compatible). Set the AWS_* vars
#             below from `railway bucket credentials`.
#
# IMPORTANT (issue #285): web and worker are two processes. The web process
# writes the upload, the worker reads it back to extract it. With
# STORAGE_DRIVER=local they MUST share the same directory on a persistent
# volume — docker-compose.yml mounts one at /app/uploads for both services.
#
# On Railway the app and the worker are SEPARATE services on separate machines
# and cannot share a disk, so STORAGE_DRIVER=local there means every extraction
# fails to find its file. Deployed environments must use 'railway'.
STORAGE_DRIVER=local
UPLOADS_DIR=uploads

# [required-prod] [worker] — only when STORAGE_DRIVER=railway.
# Get with: railway bucket credentials
AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
AWS_S3_BUCKET_NAME=
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=
AWS_S3_URL_STYLE=path


# --- Reverse proxy / client IP ------------------------------------------------
# [prod-only, proxy-dependent] — leave commented out for the included
# docker-compose.yml: it publishes port 3000 straight to the host with no
# proxy in front, so getClientAddress() must read the direct socket peer.
#
# adapter-node resolves getClientAddress() from the socket peer unless
# ADDRESS_HEADER names a header to trust instead. Only set it where something
# in front of this process actually terminates TLS and *overwrites* that
# header on every request — a real reverse proxy you run yourself (nginx,
# Caddy) or a managed platform whose edge does this for you (Railway does;
# confirm before assuming it of others). XFF_DEPTH is how many trusted hops
# sit between the app and the client — set it to that exact count.
#
# Get this wrong in either direction and IP-keyed rate limits break:
#   - unset behind a real proxy: every visitor shares one bucket, so a few
#     users trip the login/signup/waitlist limits for everyone (issue #223)
#   - set with nothing in front to rewrite it: any client can put whatever
#     IP it wants in the header and bypass those same limits (issue #500)
#
# Uncomment only when deploying behind one of the trusted proxies above.
# ADDRESS_HEADER=x-forwarded-for
# XFF_DEPTH=1


# --- Gemini AI ----------------------------------------------------------------
# [required] [worker] — extraction fails without it; env.ts logs a warning at boot.
# Any non-empty value works locally if you are not exercising extraction.
GEMINI_API_KEY=
# [optional] Update here when Google deprecates the current model.
# https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
# [optional] Per-request wall-clock timeout for extraction (ms). When it fires,
# the in-flight Gemini request is aborted (not just abandoned) so it stops
# holding a socket and a Gemini concurrency slot. Lower it during a Gemini
# incident to fail fast instead of backing the worker up.
GEMINI_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# [optional] LLM provider seam. 'gemini' is the only implemented value; any
# other name throws at createLLMProvider(). Leave unset.
# LLM_PROVIDER=gemini


# --- Invoice extraction -------------------------------------------------------
# [optional] [worker]
# GLOBAL cap on parallel Gemini extraction calls, enforced by acquireExtractionSlot()
# in rate-limiter.ts. With Upstash Redis configured the cap holds across every
# worker process (distributed, lease/TTL guarded); without it, it is per-process.
# Default 3 lets a small batch (e.g. 3 one-page invoices) extract in parallel so
# users don't wait through them one at a time. Raise it only after measuring
# against your Gemini rate/quota (higher = faster throughput but more 429/cost
# risk); set it to 1 to force strictly-sequential behaviour. The worker's
# pg-boss batchSize follows this value.
MAX_CONCURRENT_EXTRACTIONS=3

# [optional] [web] Stall handling for queued extractions (issue #540). Web-side
# on purpose: the failure these catch is "the worker is not running", so a
# worker-side sweeper would be down at the same moment.
# WARN: how long an item may sit queued before /batch/[id] replaces the spinner
# with a "taking longer than expected" card offering Retry.
# TIMEOUT: hard cutoff — an item still queued/extracting past this is marked
# failed with extract.err.stalled and inherits the existing Retry/Discard UI.
# Keep TIMEOUT above the worst legitimate run (pg-boss retryLimit 2 x retryDelay
# 30s, each attempt bounded by GEMINI_TIMEOUT_MS) or a working extraction gets
# reaped and its result discarded.
EXTRACTION_STALL_WARN_MS=120000
EXTRACTION_STALL_TIMEOUT_MS=900000

# [optional] [worker/web] Worker liveness heartbeat (issue #540). The worker
# upserts worker_heartbeats every INTERVAL; /admin/health and /api/health call it
# down once the row is older than STALE. Queue depth alone cannot tell "worker
# dead" from "worker busy" — this is what disambiguates it. STALE must stay
# comfortably above INTERVAL or a healthy worker flaps.
WORKER_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS=30000
WORKER_HEARTBEAT_STALE_MS=120000


# --- Rate limiting / distributed coordination ---------------------------------
# [required-prod when running more than one replica] [worker]
# Upstash Redis backs rate limiting across multiple web instances AND the
# extraction concurrency semaphore across multiple worker processes. If unset,
# both fall back to in-memory / in-process state, which is correct only for a
# single instance.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# [optional] Chat requests per minute per IP.
CHAT_RATE_LIMIT_RPM=20

# [optional] Global backstop for /api/* — requests per minute per user (or per
# IP when unauthenticated) before hooks.server.ts answers 429. Per-route limits
# are stricter and fire first; this only catches routes without one. The health
# check and the signature-verified Stripe/WhatsApp webhooks are exempt.
# 0 disables the backstop entirely.
API_GLOBAL_RATE_LIMIT=300

# [optional] /api/health is public (load balancers hit it unauthenticated)
# but its own dedicated per-IP limit, not the backstop above — requests/minute
# before it answers 429. Default is generous for probe cadence (issue #491).
HEALTH_RATE_LIMIT_RPM=60

# [optional] Shared secret that unlocks the detailed /api/health response (DB
# size, queue depth, uploads-dir, sessions, uptime, version) for external
# monitoring, via an `X-Health-Token` header. Unset = only an admin session
# can see the detail; the public response stays `{ status }` either way
# (issue #491).
# HEALTH_CHECK_TOKEN=


# --- Bot protection (Cloudflare Turnstile) -------------------------------------
# [optional] CAPTCHA on the public signup and waitlist forms, on top of the
# always-on honeypot field and IP rate limits. Both keys unset = feature off:
# the widget does not render and the server skips verification. Both set = the
# widget renders and submissions without a valid token are rejected (422).
# Keys: https://dash.cloudflare.com → Turnstile. Set BOTH or NEITHER — a secret
# without a site key rejects every signup, because no widget ever renders.
# If the siteverify API is unreachable the check fails open (availability over
# bot-blocking). PUBLIC_ prefix: the site key ships to the browser by design.
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
# PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=


# --- Observability ------------------------------------------------------------
# [optional] Leave empty to disable Sentry (safe and expected for local dev).
SENTRY_DSN=
VITE_SENTRY_DSN=
# [optional] Auth token for source map upload (build-time) and the /admin/errors
# + /admin/health Sentry API calls (runtime). Create in Sentry: Settings → Auth
# Tokens, scopes project:read, event:read, org:read. Leave empty to disable both
# — both degrade to a "not configured" state rather than erroring.
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
SENTRY_ORG=
SENTRY_PROJECT=
# [optional] Sentry API base URL for the /admin/errors + /admin/health runtime
# calls (listUnresolvedIssues / getIssueSummary). Defaults to the EU region —
# this deployment's org lives there. A US-region (or other non-EU) org must
# change this to https://sentry.io/api/0, or those calls 401/404 even though
# isSentryConfigured() reports true.
SENTRY_API_BASE_URL=https://de.sentry.io/api/0
# [optional] Set to the deployed git SHA so Sentry can bisect issues to a release.
SENTRY_RELEASE=
VITE_SENTRY_RELEASE=


# --- Billing (Stripe) ---------------------------------------------------------
# [required-prod] dashboard.stripe.com → Developers → API keys
# Local dev: always sk_test_... here. Live keys (rk_live_.../sk_live_...) go in
# Railway's variable store, never in this file or .env.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
# [required-prod] One monthly price ID per paid tier (Products → Price ID).
# All three are required for their tier's checkout button to work; an unset tier
# returns a "plan not available" error on /billing instead of 500 (issue #286).
# Rotating a price in Stripe means updating it here too — the webhook logs an
# error (and Sentry event) when a subscription arrives with an unknown price ID.
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_STARTER=price_...
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_PRO=price_...
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_BUSINESS=price_...
# [optional] Legacy single-price variable — fallback for the Starter tier only.
STRIPE_PRICE_ID=price_...
# [required-prod] Webhook signing secret. Set the endpoint to
# {your-origin}/api/stripe-webhook. Unset in production, webhook signature
# verification cannot run and subscription state silently stops updating.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
# [optional] Coupon applied at checkout for private-beta founders (users approved
# from /admin/access while the app is closed). Create it in the Stripe dashboard
# and paste its ID here. Unset: founders still get the longer trial, no discount.
STRIPE_FOUNDER_COUPON_ID=
# [optional] Monthly list price per tier, in euros, used by /admin/revenue to
# price MRR. Unset, each tier falls back to PROVISIONAL_PRICE in
# src/lib/billing-plans.ts (the same table the public pricing page renders).
# Set these when the billed price diverges from the published one, otherwise
# MRR, ARPA, ACV, LTV and the LTV/CAC ratio are all computed off a stale number.
PLAN_PRICE_STARTER_EUR=
PLAN_PRICE_PRO_EUR=
PLAN_PRICE_BUSINESS_EUR=


# --- Email (Resend) -----------------------------------------------------------
# [required-prod] resend.com → API Keys. Unset, verification and password-reset
# mail is never delivered.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# [required-prod] Verified sender domain; update after domain verification.
EMAIL_FROM=Mise en Place <noreply@miseenplace.app>
# [optional] Legal footer on outgoing emails (razón social, dirección fiscal, NIF).
# Left blank, that footer line is omitted rather than shown with placeholder text.
COMPANY_LEGAL_NAME=
COMPANY_ADDRESS=
COMPANY_NIF=


# --- WhatsApp Cloud API (Meta) ------------------------------------------------
# [optional as a feature — but all five are required together once enabled]
# Set up at: developers.facebook.com/apps → WhatsApp → API Setup
#   Webhook URL: https://your-domain.com/api/whatsapp/webhook
#   Subscribed fields: messages
# Authorise staff numbers in the app: Settings → "Facturas por WhatsApp" (owner only).
#
# WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN    — Permanent system user token (keep secret)
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID — Phone Number ID from the API Setup page
# WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN    — Any secret string; verifies the webhook in Meta Console
# WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET      — App secret (App Dashboard → Settings → Basic). Verifies
#                            X-Hub-Signature-256 on inbound POSTs. REQUIRED in
#                            production; without it the webhook cannot authenticate Meta.
# WHATSAPP_API_VERSION     — Graph API version (default v25.0). Meta expires each
#                            version ~2 years after release and calls to an expired
#                            one fail outright — review this at every upgrade.
# WHATSAPP_DISPLAY_NUMBER  — The bot's own number, in a dialable form (e.g.
#                            +34 612 345 678). WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID is an
#                            opaque id, so without this the app cannot tell staff
#                            where to send invoices — Settings shows the number,
#                            a wa.me link and a printable QR only when it is set.
# WHATSAPP_SENDER_HOURLY_LIMIT — Invoices one authorised number may send per hour
#                            (default 30). Guards the extraction queue and the LLM
#                            quota against a burst from a single sender.
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=
WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=
WHATSAPP_API_VERSION=v25.0
WHATSAPP_DISPLAY_NUMBER=
WHATSAPP_SENDER_HOURLY_LIMIT=30

# [optional] [worker] Set to 'true' on the WORKER service to start the Baileys
# WhatsApp client (ADR-025) — the MVP transport, used while the business is not
# yet registered and Meta Cloud API credentials are unobtainable. Independent of
# the WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN / WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID pair above: those drive
# the Cloud API webhook, this drives the socket the worker holds.
#
# Pairing is by QR, shown in the worker log and at /admin/whatsapp. The session
# is stored in the whatsapp_session table, so no volume is needed.
#
# Use a DEDICATED number, never a personal one — WhatsApp bans numbers using
# unofficial clients without notice. /admin/whatsapp has a kill switch that stops
# the bot without a redeploy; this variable is the harder gate above it.
# WHATSAPP_BOT_ENABLED=true

# [required-prod] Public origin used to build the /batch/[id] link sent back over
# WhatsApp when an inbound invoice is received (e.g. https://app.miseenplace.app).
# env.ts warns at boot when unset; links degrade to relative paths.
APP_BASE_URL=


# --- Initial admin account (seeded on first db:seed run) ----------------------
# [optional] Local convenience only. auth-seed throws in production when the
# password is still "changeme" or the email is an @example.* placeholder — but
# that throw is currently swallowed by the caller's .catch() (issue #509), so
# treat it as a reminder, not a guarantee. Set real values or leave unset.
AUTH_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
AUTH_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme
AUTH_ADMIN_RESTAURANT_NAME=My Restaurant

# --- Browser QA sweep (`pnpm qa:sweep`) — optional, dev-only ---
# Not read by the app. Every one has a default, so the sweep runs with none of
# them set; override only when the defaults do not match your setup.
# QA_BASE_URL — origin to sweep. Vite moves to 5174+ when 5173 is taken, so check
#               the dev-server log rather than assuming (default http://localhost:5173).
# QA_EMAIL / QA_PASSWORD — account used for the authenticated pass
#               (default: AUTH_ADMIN_EMAIL / AUTH_ADMIN_PASSWORD above).
# QA_OUT      — report path (default qa-report.md, gitignored).
# QA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173
# QA_EMAIL=
# QA_PASSWORD=
# QA_OUT=qa-report.md
